{ Lasker plans, from Edward Lasker's book on strategy. Find the piece with no future and open a road for it: today, a pawn break on d5. [%cal Yd7d5] } 3... d5 { Your bishop on c8 finally gets a diagonal. Development comes before pawn hunting. [%cal Gd7d5] } (3... Nc6 { Your knight develops to c6, but it leaves the bishop on c8 boxed in a while longer. } 4. cxd4 { He takes the pawn back, and your bishop on c8 still looks at nothing but your own pawns on b7 and d7. [%csl Rc8] }) (3... c5 { You prop the extra pawn up with c5. That keeps the pawn — and your bishop on c8 is still staring at your own men. } 4. Nf3 { He develops instead of chasing the pawn, and your bishop is boxed in exactly as it was. [%cal Gd7d5] }) (3... dxc3 { You take the second pawn. Greedy and good — but every line you open is a line his pieces reach first. } 4. Nxc3 { He takes back and has a free hand in the middle. You are a pawn up with three pieces still at home. [%cal Gd7d5] }) (3... Qe7 { The queen steps out to e7 and looks up the open column toward his king. } 4. cxd4 4... Qxe4+ 5. Ne2 { You do win a pawn with check. But she has to move again soon, and the break on d5 frees a piece instead. [%cal Gd7d5] }) (3... Ne7 { The knight steps out to e7, where it takes the square your dark-squared bishop wanted. } 4. Nf3 { Your knight on e7 now blocks the bishop on f8, and the bishop on c8 is still shut in. Two pieces stuck instead of one. [%csl Rc8,Rf8] }) (3... Nf6 { A natural developing move — but with pawns on d4 and e4 he can chase this knight around at his leisure. } 4. e5 4... Nd5 5. Qxd4 { He develops the queen while collecting d4. } 5... Nb6 { Your knight has hopped three times to reach b6, and the bishop on c8 is still behind the pawn on d7. [%csl Rc8] }) 4. exd5 { He trades the pawn attacking e4, not the one on d4. Recapture with your queen. [%cal Yd8d5] } 4... Qxd5 { Your queen recaptures, and the diagonal is finally clear for the bishop. [%tail] [%cal Gd8d5] } (4... dxc3 { You take a second pawn instead of recapturing. Material is nice, and the bishop on c8 is still shut in behind its pawns. } 5. Nxc3 { He takes back and develops. Your queen never reached d5, the square that opens the bishop's road. [%cal Gd8d5] }) (4... Bd6 { Your dark-squared bishop develops to d6, though it's the other bishop on c8 that needed the open road. } 5. Nf3 { He develops in comfort. The bishop that needed help is the one on c8, and it is still waiting. [%cal Gd8d5] }) (4... Be7 { Your dark-squared bishop settles on e7, while the light-squared one on c8 was still the piece needing room. } 5. Nf3 { He brings a piece out. Your dark bishop has a square either way; the light one has none until the queen takes on d5. [%cal Gd8d5] }) (4... Nf6 { You develop first and leave his pawn on d5 alone. It is a real move, but the queen recapture is what clears the bishop's diagonal. } 5. Bb5+ { He develops with check before collecting d4. } 5... Nbd7 6. Qxd4 { His queen collects d4 and he is a pawn up, while your knight on d7 blocks the bishop on c8 once more. [%csl Rc8] }) 5. cxd4 5... Nc6 6. Nf3 6... Bg4 { Your bishop is finally out, pinning the knight to the queen behind it — the diagonal you opened three moves ago. [%cal Gc8g4] } 0-1


[Event "Lasker plans: Meet the Vienna in the center"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_f3_0_3"]
