[ChapterId "S03"]
[Domain "strategy"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Central pawn breaks"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter IV, French pawn chains"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ A pawn chain points at your king side. Can you find the pawn break that attacks its base instead of its tip? } 3... c5 { You attack d4, the support of the advanced e pawn. [%cal Gc7c5] } (3... a6 { Your pawn steps to a6, but it adds no pressure against d4. } 4. c3 { He props the base of his chain up in peace. Your c pawn is the one that puts the question to d4. [%cal Gc7c5] }) (3... Nc6 { This knight stands in your own c pawn's doorway. } 4. c3 { He builds his chain in comfort. With a knight on c6 the c pawn can never come, and d4 is never attacked at all. [%cal Gc7c5] }) (3... Bd7 { Your bishop settles on d7, developing but leaving d4 unpressured for now. } 4. Nf3 { The bishop has a home, and his pawn on d4 has not been asked a single question. [%cal Gc7c5] }) (3... Ne7 { Your knight steps out to e7, though it still is not the piece leaning on d4. } 4. b4 { He takes space on the other wing while the base of his chain stands untouched. [%cal Gc7c5] }) (3... Nh6 { Your knight heads for the rim on h6, away from the fight for d4. } 4. c3 { A knight on the rim, and the pawn that hits the base of his chain is still at home. [%cal Gc7c5] }) 4. c3 { He adds a pawn to defend the base. } 4... Nc6 { Your knight joins the pressure your pawn break started. The chain needs constant care now. [%cal Gb8c6] } (4... cxd4 { Your pawn trades on d4 early, handing him an easy recapture. } 5. cxd4 { Trading early hands him an easy recapture. Keep the tension and add a knight to the attack on d4 instead. [%cal Gb8c6] }) (4... Bd7 { Your bishop settles on d7 instead, developing but adding no fresh pressure on d4. } 5. Nf3 { A useful piece to develop — but a knight on c6 is what puts a second attacker on d4. [%cal Gb8c6] }) (4... Qb6 { The queen leans on d4 too, and that is a real plan. The knight usually goes first, so she is not left out there alone. } 5. Nf3 { He develops calmly. Knight to c6 first adds the same pressure and keeps the queen's choices open. [%cal Gb8c6] }) (4... Ne7 { Your knight develops to e7, a slower route to the pressure that c6 gives it. } 5. Nf3 { This knight route is the slow one. From c6 it presses on d4 straight away. [%cal Gb8c6] }) (4... Nh6 { Your knight heads for the rim on h6, still not the piece that presses on d4. } 5. Bb5+ 5... Bd7 6. Bxd7+ 6... Nxd7 { You develop the knight with the recapture, rather than moving your queen or king. } 7. Bxh6 7... gxh6 { A pair of minor pieces has come off, and the real work against d4 still belongs to the c pawn and a knight on c6. [%cal Gb8c6] }) 0-1


[Event "Lasker plans: Open the center against an uncastled king"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqr1k1/ppppbppp/2n2n2/8/2B1P3/PQ5P/1PP2PP1/RNB1K1NR_b_KQ_-_0_8"]
