{ You want to attack the d pawn. Can you fix the target before lining up your pieces? } 10. cxd5 { You exchange first, so his central pawn becomes a lasting target. [%cal Gc4d5] } (10. Rc1 { The rook takes the half-open column. Useful — but his pawn on d5 can still trade itself off whenever he likes. } 10... dxc4 11. Bxc4 { He settles the middle on his own terms, and the target you wanted to fix has walked away. [%cal Gc4d5] }) (10. Nxd5 { That pawn is defended. The knight capture loses material. } 10... exd5 11. cxd5 11... Nxd5 { His knight takes back on d5, and you are a knight down for one pawn. Take with the c pawn instead. [%cal Rf6d5] }) (10. Qc2 { The queen takes a good square. The pawn on d5 is still free to choose its own moment to trade. } 10... dxc4 11. Bxc4 { He trades on c4 himself, and there is no fixed pawn left for your pieces to attack. [%cal Gc4d5] }) (10. Qd2 { The queen aims down the d column at a pawn that can still walk away from her. } 10... dxc4 { And away it goes. Fix the pawn first, then aim at it. [%cal Gc4d5] }) (10. Be2 { Natural development. It leaves his pawn on d5 free to trade itself off at a moment of his choosing. } 10... dxc4 11. Bxc4 { He takes on c4 and the pawns settle the way he wanted them. Trade first and his pawn is stuck on d5 for good. [%cal Gc4d5] }) (10. h3 { A little air for your king, and not a word said to the pawn in the middle. } 10... dxc4 11. Bxc4 { The pawn trades itself off and your target has gone. The exchange on d5 nails it in place. [%cal Gc4d5] }) 10... exd5 { He recaptures and keeps material level. } 11. Be2 { Your bishop develops without stepping in front of the d column. [%tail] [%cal Gf1e2] } (11. Rc1 { The rook comes to c1, which is fine in itself. The question here is where the bishop goes — d3 blocks the rooks, e2 does not. } 11... Ne5 { Useful enough. A bishop on e2 develops and leaves the d column free for a rook. [%cal Gf1e2] }) (11. Qd2 { The queen steps onto the very column your rook wants for itself. } 11... a5 { She has a square, and now the rook has to queue behind her. From e2 the bishop keeps that road clear. [%cal Gf1e2] }) (11. Bd3 { The bishop develops — onto the one square where it blocks its own rook. } 11... Ne5 { Your bishop is parked on the rook's road. On e2 it does the same job and stays out of the way. [%cal Gf1e2] }) (11. h3 { Another quiet pawn move while the bishop still has to choose its square. } 11... Ne5 { Quiet and harmless. A bishop on e2 develops and keeps the d column clear for the rook. [%cal Gf1e2] }) 11... Be6 12. O-O 12... Qe7 13. Rc1 { Your rook joins the open column your exchange created, piling one more attacker onto his position. [%cal Ga1c1] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker plans: Shut the bishop out"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1b1r1k1/1pp1n1pp/p1pb1p2/8/4PP2/1NN5/PPP3PP/R1B2RK1_w_-_-_0_12"]
