{ Your pieces have more room. Can a pawn move make his bishop on c8 even less comfortable? } 12. f5 { Your pawn takes e6 away from the bishop and frees f4 for your own bishop. [%cal Gf4f5] } (12. Be3 { You develop before restricting his bishop. } 12... Nd5 13. exd5 13... Rxe3 14. dxc6 14... bxc6 { He retains more freedom. The f5 pawn move denies his bishop e6. [%cal Rb7c6] }) 12... b6 { He looks for another diagonal. } 13. Bf4 { Your bishop offers a useful trade. His cramped pieces still need room. [%tail] [%cal Gc1f4] } (13. a3 { A little air for your king. His bishop on d6 is still there to be traded, and f4 is the square that offers it. } 13... Bb7 { A useful pause. Your bishop still wants f4, where it offers the trade and takes e5 away from him. [%cal Gc1f4] }) (13. Be3 { You retreat from the useful bishop trade. } 13... Nxf5 14. Bxb6 14... Ng3 { He leaves the bishop on b6 and attacks your rook with the knight. } 15. Bxc7 15... Bxc7 { He saves his attacked bishop by taking on c7 instead of taking your rook. } 16. hxg3 { Recapturing with the pawn gives him the easier game — from f4 your bishop would have challenged his dark-squared bishop instead. [%cal Rh2g3] }) (13. Rf2 { The rook tidies itself up behind the pawn. The bishop on c1 is the piece with a job waiting. } 13... c5 { The rook has a square, and your bishop still has not taken the one that matters. [%cal Gc1f4] }) (13. g3 { A quiet pawn move beside your king. It takes nothing away from him. } 13... Bb7 { Nothing has changed for either side. From f4 your bishop would be offering the trade that leaves him cramped. [%cal Gc1f4] }) (13. g4 { Space on the wing. His cramped pieces are happy to watch you spend moves out there. } 13... Bb7 { He gets on with his own plans, and the trade on f4 is still the move that keeps him short of room. [%cal Gc1f4] }) 13... Bxf4 14. Rxf4 { The cramped bishop is off the board, and you keep the more active minor pieces. [%cal Gf1f4] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker plans: Keep the doubled pawns stuck"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/b5k1/4bppp/2p1p3/q1ppB1P1/5P2/1P1PPN2/2P1Q2P/6K1_w_-_-_1_20"]
