{ Now let us remove the defender. His bishop on g7 guards the dark squares around his king — trade it off with Bh6. [%cal Ye3h6,Gh6g7] } 2. Bh6 { Your bishop offers to remove the guard of the dark squares. [%cal Ge3h6] } (2. Bf4 { The bishop takes a decent square. It leaves the bishop on g7, the piece guarding every dark square near his king. } 2... dxe5 3. dxe5 { You have your center back, and his best defender is still sitting on g7. [%cal Ge3h6] }) (2. exd6 { Your pawn captures on d6, restoring the center, but it delays removing his dark-square guard. } 2... cxd6 { The pawns are level again, and the trade on g7 has to wait — he may not offer it a second time. [%cal Ge3h6] }) 2... dxe5 { He grabs a pawn instead. Take his bishop on g7 before restoring your center. [%cal Yh6g7] } 3. Bxg7 { His dark-square guard is gone, and his king will feel that for the rest of the game. [%cal Gh6g7] } (3. dxe5 { Taking back first is fine too. The bishop on h6 is not going anywhere, and neither is his on g7. } 3... Nb4 { Your center is restored, and the bishop on h6 still has its job to do. [%cal Gh6g7] }) 3... Kxg7 { His king recaptures. Take the pawn on e5 with your d pawn. [%cal Yd4e5] } 4. dxe5 { You restore your center. His dark squares no longer have their bishop guard. [%cal Gd4e5] } (4. Nxe5 { The knight recapture leaves your pawn on d4 exposed. } 4... Nxd4 { He takes d4 with the knight, gaining a central post without exposing his queen. } 5. Rad1 5... c5 { His c pawn guards the knight on d4, and you are simply a pawn down. Taking back with the d pawn kept it. [%cal Rc7c5] }) 1-0


[Event "Lasker plans: Give your cramped bishop a job"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqk2r/pppnbppp/4p3/3pP1B1/3P4/2N5/PPP2PPP/R2QKBNR_w_KQkq_-_1_6"]
