[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Choose useful exchanges"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter IV, Diagram 34 discussion"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]

{ Your pawn chain cramps one bishop. Can you exchange it for a more useful enemy bishop? } 6. Bxe7 { You trade the bishop your pawns hem in for his bishop on e7. [%cal Gg5e7] } (6. Be3 { Your bishop steps back to e3, solid, but it stays boxed by your own pawn chain a while longer. } 6... c5 { A sound alternative — and the bishop it leaves behind is the one with no future at all. [%cal Gg5e7] }) (6. Bf4 { You keep the bishop behind your own pawn chain. } 6... c5 7. dxc5 7... Nc6 { He develops his other knight before taking on c5, and your cramped bishop still has no job. [%cal Gg5e7] }) (6. h4 { Your pawn advances to h4, provoking a response rather than trading the cramped bishop right away. } 6... h6 { He attacks your bishop with a pawn instead of exchanging immediately on g5. } 7. Bxe7 7... Qxe7 { The bishops come off anyway, only after two pawn moves on the h column. Nothing lost, nothing gained. [%cal Gg5e7] }) 6... Qxe7 { His queen steps in to recapture. } 7. f4 { Your f pawn now guards e5, so the chain holds on its own, and your bad bishop is already gone. [%cal Gf2f4][%csl Ge5] } (7. Nb5 { The knight steps aside so the c pawn can prop up d4. A different way to hold your chain together. } 7... Nb6 { His knight on b6 now eyes c4, and your pawn on e5 still has nothing behind it. A pawn on f4 would hold it. [%cal Gf2f4] }) (7. Qd2 { The queen prepares to bring the knight back and play c3. Reasonable — and slower than simply backing e5 with a pawn. } 7... O-O { This looks after d4 in time. The pawn to f4 looks after e5 and takes space in the same move. [%cal Gf2f4] }) (7. Bd3 { Your bishop develops to d3, natural, though f4 is what actually backs up the e5 pawn. } 7... Qb4 { His queen jumps to b4 and hits your pawn on b2, while your pawn on e5 still stands with no pawn behind it. [%csl Rb2] }) (7. Nf3 { Your knight heads to f3 right away, but it blocks the very f4 push that backs up e5. } 7... O-O { Your own knight now sits on the square the pawn wanted. Pawn first, knight afterwards. [%cal Gf2f4] }) (7. h4 { Your pawn advances on the wing, but f4 is still the move that reinforces e5. } 7... O-O { Space on the wing, and your pawn on e5 still has nothing behind it. [%cal Gf2f4] }) 1-0


