{ You can exchange minor pieces. Which trade avoids helping a pawn chase your other knight? } 6. Bxe7 { You exchange bishops without drawing his d pawn onto e4. [%cal Gg5e7] } (6. Nxe4 { You take the knight first. Sound enough — but his pawn takes back on e4, and from there it covers f3. [%csl Bf3] } 6... dxe4 { He restores material with the d pawn rather than starting a bishop exchange. } 7. Bxe7 7... Qxe7 { His pawn on e4 now covers f3, so your knight on g1 has lost its best square. [%csl Rf3] }) (6. Qa4+ { Your queen checks from a4, active, but trading bishops first avoids drawing his pawn to e4. } 6... Bd7 7. Bxe7 7... Qxe7 { His bishop blocked on d7, and your queen now stands on a4, far from the centre she was meant to hold. [%csl Ba4] }) (6. Bf4 { Your bishop settles behind the pawns on f4, safe, but it lets his center pawn reach e4 all the same. } 6... c5 7. cxd5 7... Nxc3 { He trades knights before recovering the d5 pawn. } 8. bxc3 8... Qa5 { He pins c3 before recovering d5 — trading bishops immediately sidesteps that extra pressure. [%cal Bd8a5] }) (6. h4 { Your pawn grabs space with h4, but it still invites the same pawn advance to e4. } 6... Bxg5 7. hxg5 7... Nxc3 { He exchanges knights before collecting the pawn on g5. } 8. bxc3 8... Qxg5 { His queen takes your pawn on g5, and you are a pawn down for the open h column. [%cal Rd8g5] }) 6... Qxe7 { His queen recaptures, ready to meet whatever comes next. } 7. Qc2 { Your queen supports the center, and f3 is still open for your other knight. [%tail] [%cal Gd1c2] } (7. Rc1 { The rook takes the half-open column, a good square in itself. The queen does more from c2: she holds the center and keeps f3 free for your knight. } 7... O-O { You organize differently, and it is fine. The queen to c2 is the move that keeps his pawn off e4. [%cal Gd1c2] }) (7. Nxe4 { Your knight grabs the pawn on e4, but it hands him a pawn there that controls f3. [%csl Bf3] } 7... dxe4 { His pawn recaptures and now controls f3 — not a tactical mistake, but the queen move to c2 keeps f3 free instead. [%cal Bd5e4][%csl Bf3] }) (7. cxd5 { Also good — you settle the center at once, and give up the tension a move earlier than you needed to. } 7... Nxc3 8. bxc3 8... exd5 { A useful plan too. The queen to c2 keeps more pawns on the board and f3 free for the knight. [%cal Gd1c2] }) (7. Qb3 { The queen chooses b3 instead. Active — and from c2 she looks straight down the diagonal at his knight on e4. } 7... dxc4 8. Bxc4 { You develop the bishop with the recapture instead of moving your queen again. } 8... Nxc3 9. Qxc3 { You take back with the queen and keep your pawns whole. From c2 she was already eyeing e4. [%cal Gd1c2] }) (7. Bd3 { The bishop takes d3 — and stands right on the diagonal your queen wanted toward e4. } 7... Nxc3 8. bxc3 8... dxc4 9. Bxc4 { Your own pieces are in each other's way. From c2 the queen looks straight at his knight. [%cal Gc2e4] }) (7. Nge2 { The knight develops to e2. A modest square, and it gives up f3 — the very square this chapter is saving for it. } 7... Nxc3 8. Nxc3 { Your last knight is out, on the square that does less. Keep f3 free and it goes there instead. [%cal Gg1f3] }) (7. Nf3 { Straight to f3, and it works too. Playing the queen to c2 first is what makes sure the square stays free. } 7... Nxc3 8. bxc3 { He trades knights and your pawns are doubled. With the queen already on c2 she takes back instead of a pawn. [%cal Gd1c2] }) 7... Nxc3 8. Qxc3 8... O-O 9. Nf3 { Your last knight finally reaches f3 — the square you took such care not to block three moves ago. [%cal Gg1f3] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker plans: A pawn for the open file"]
