{ Now let us activate a rook: put it on an open column — no pawns blocking it — where it can run freely. Move it to b8; the c pawn buys time to invade. [%cal Ya8b8] } 1... Rb8 { Your rook gets an open road instead of tying the bishop to a pawn. [%cal Ga8b8] } (1... a5 { Your pawn steps to a5, gaining space, but it leaves the rook's open column unclaimed. } 2. Rfe1 { He takes an open column of his own while your rook is still at home. [%cal Ga8b8] }) (1... Bb7 { The bishop steps out to guard the pawn. That is the opposite of the plan: the pawn is the price you pay for the open column. } 2. h3 { The bishop is out, and your rook still has not taken the column. [%cal Ga8b8] }) (1... Bd7 { The bishop defends the pawn from d7. Same idea, same cost — a piece tied to a pawn, and a column left unclaimed. } 2. Rfd1 { You saved the pawn and gave up the road. Letting it go is what buys the rook the open column. [%cal Ga8b8] }) (1... Be6 { Your bishop develops to e6, natural, but the rook still wants the open b column for itself. } 2. Rfe1 { He takes a column of his own while yours is still unclaimed. [%cal Ga8b8] }) (1... Bf5 { Your bishop develops actively to f5, but the rook invasion down the open column comes first. } 2. Bxc6 { He takes the pawn on c6, and your rook on a8 still has not reached the open b column. [%cal Ga8b8] }) (1... Rd8 { The rook picks the d column, where his own rook can come and meet it. The b column is the one with nothing in the way. } 2. Rfe1 { He grabs the e column, and your rook is on a road that is already blocked. [%cal Ga8b8] }) 2. Bxc6 { He takes the pawn. Send your rook to b2 and make the open column count. [%cal Yb8b2] } 2... Rb2 { Your rook enters on b2 and attacks the pawns from the side. [%cal Gb8b2] } (2... Be6 { You bring the bishop out first. The road is open now, and open roads have a habit of closing. } 3. Be4 { A fair order. Your rook still wants the second row, where it hits his pawns from the side. [%cal Gb8b2] }) (2... Bf5 { The bishop swings out to f5, active — and the rook is one move from landing behind his pawns. } 3. Bd5 3... Bxc2 { Your bishop wins the pawn back on c2 and material is level. Your rook is still waiting on b8 instead of biting on the second row. [%cal Gb8b2] }) (2... Rd8 { Your rook leaves the open column it just took, for one his rook can contest. } 3. Ba4 { Your rook left the b column it had just taken, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%cal Gb8b2] }) (2... g6 { A little air for your king, and the rook is still one move from the second row. } 3. Bd5 { Harmless enough, though the invasion needed no extra pawn move to prepare it. [%cal Gb8b2] }) 0-1


[Event "Lasker plans: Prepare to invade on the c column"]
