{ Your knight has broken in. Which rook move adds pressure along the open c column? } 33... Rc6 { Your rook claims the c column, joining the knight that has already broken in. [%tail] [%cal Ga6c6] } (33... b4 { The pawn takes space. With a knight already inside his position, the rook on the open column is worth far more. } 34. Rdxc2 34... dxc2+ 35. Qxc2 { Still winning, only slower. Both rooks belong on the column that leads to his king. [%cal Ga6c6] }) (33... Na3+ { A check for its own sake. His king steps aside and your knight is no longer sitting in the middle of his camp. } 34. Ka1 { He steps into the corner and nothing has changed. The rook was the piece with a free road. [%cal Ga6c6] }) (33... Rd4 { The rook takes a central square, where his own pieces can come and challenge it. } 34. Rdxc2 34... dxc2+ 35. Qxc2 { Winning, but the open column was the straight road in. [%cal Ga6c6] }) (33... Kh8 { A safe little king move, in a position where it is his king that is in trouble. } 34. Rxd3 34... Rxd3 35. Qxc2 { Nothing lost by it, and nothing collected. That rook belongs on the open column. [%cal Ga6c6] }) 34. Qe5 34... Rdc8 { Your other rook joins in — the c column is fully yours, with both rooks aimed at his king. [%cal Gd8c8] } 0-1


[Event "Lasker plans: Trade into control of the e column"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r3k1/pp1qrpp1/7p/2p2Q1P/3pR3/3P4/PPP2PP1/4R1K1_w_-_-_0_1"]
