{ You own the only open column. Can you exchange pieces without giving up that road? } 1. Qxd7 { The queens come off, and the open column is still yours. [%cal Gf5d7] } (1. Rxe7 { The rook capture leaves your queen hanging. } 1... Qxf5 2. R1e5 { You attack the queen before taking b7, but your material loss remains. } 2... Qg4 { You lose the queen for too little. Trade queens before trading rooks. [%cal Rf5g4] }) (1. Qf3 { The queen steps across to f3, active enough. Trading on d7 removes his best piece and keeps the column yours. } 1... Rce8 { A fair try. The queen trade first is what leaves nothing of his to contest the column. [%cal Gf5d7] }) (1. g4 { Your pawn steps forward on g4, but the queen trade is what keeps the e column safely yours. } 1... Rxe4 2. Qxd7 { You take the queen on d7, not the rook on e4. The bigger prize comes first. } 2... Rxe1+ 3. Kg2 { You have a queen against two rooks — trading down to a simpler rook ending was the calmer path. [%cal Bg1g2] }) 1... Rxd7 { The rook recaptures. } 2. Re8+ { Your first rook offers another exchange with check. [%cal Ge4e8] } (2. a4 { A pawn move on the queen side. Nothing wrong with it — but the check is what makes the trade happen on your terms. } 2... b6 { He waits too, and the rooks still face each other. Check first and the exchange goes your way. [%cal Ge4e8] }) (2. b3 { Another quiet pawn move while the rooks stand nose to nose. } 2... b6 { He is in no hurry either. The check on e8 settles the column for good. [%cal Ge4e8] }) (2. Re7 { The rook steps onto the seventh row without a check, and that hands him a free move to answer. } 2... Rcd8 { Your rook is active, but the check would have landed it on the last row with the column still yours. [%cal Ge4e8] }) (2. g3 { A little air for your king, tidy enough, while the rooks are still unresolved. } 2... f6 { Harmless — and the check keeps a tighter grip on the column. [%cal Ge4e8] }) (2. g4 { The pawn steps forward on the wing, and the rooks are still facing each other. } 2... Kf8 { His king steps toward the centre, and the rooks still face each other on the e column. The check settles it at once. [%cal Ge4e8] }) 2... Rxe8 { He takes the checking rook. } 3. Rxe8+ { Your last rook keeps the open column. Fewer pieces, but the same useful road. [%cal Ge1e8] } (3. Kf1 { You leave the rook on e8 instead of taking it back. } 3... Rdd8 { He would have two rooks against your one. Recapture on e8 instead of moving your king. [%cal Rd7d8] }) 1-0


[Event "Lasker plans: Clear the d column for the second rook"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3rkb1r/p2nqppp/5n2/1B2p1B1/4P3/1Q6/PPP2PPP/2KR3R_w_k_-_0_13"]
