{ Can you trade the rook blocking the d column and bring its fresh partner into the attack? } 13. Rxd7 { Your rook removes a defender and clears d1 for its partner. [%cal Gd1d7] } (13. Qa4 { The queen swings out to a4. Strong enough — and it leaves the rook in the corner with nothing at all to do. } 13... Qe6 14. Bxf6 14... gxf6 { You still have a fine attack. The trade on d7 removes a defender and hands the second rook the d column. [%cal Gd1d7] }) (13. Qh3 { The queen eyes the king side. Meanwhile one rook blocks the other, and only the front one is doing any work. } 13... a6 { Still very good for you. Taking on d7 is what turns two rooks into two attackers. [%cal Gd1d7] }) (13. Bxd7+ { The bishop takes instead. That swaps your attacker for his defender and leaves the rook in the corner asleep. } 13... Rxd7 14. Rxd7 14... Qxd7 { He stays better, but the attack is weaker this way. Keep the bishops and let the rooks do the work. [%cal Re7d7] }) (13. Rd3 { The rook lifts to d3, active — and it still stands between d1 and its partner in the corner. } 13... Qc5 14. Bxd7+ 14... Rxd7 15. Qb8+ { You check with the queen instead of exchanging the rooks on d7. } 15... Ke7 { His king walks out and your attack rolls on. The rook trade first is what brings the second rook in. [%cal Gd1d7] }) (13. Rd5 { The rook advances to d5, where it looks impressive and still blocks the column for its partner. } 13... h6 14. Bxf6 14... Qxf6 { Fine for you. Taking on d7 clears the road and removes a guard in the same move. [%cal Gd1d7] }) 13... Rxd7 { He takes with the rook. That is a mistake: the knight recapture resists longer. } 14. Rd1 { Your fresh rook arrives on the open d column. The pinned knight still cannot help. [%cal Gh1d1] } (14. Qc3 { The queen centralizes. Your rook in the corner is still waiting for an invitation to d1. } 14... Qd6 { The attack is real, and a fresh rook on d1 is the piece that finishes it. [%cal Gh1d1] }) (14. Bxd7+ { This trades an attacking bishop instead of adding your rook. } 14... Qxd7 15. Qb8+ 15... Qd8 16. Qxe5+ { You take e5 with check instead of chasing the a7 pawn. } 16... Be7 { You remain better, but the attack loses force this way — the rook on h1 still needs to join in. [%cal Rf8e7] }) (14. c3 { A quiet pawn move in the middle of an attack. The second rook is still sitting in the corner. } 14... Kd8 15. Bxd7 { Still winning. The rook on h1 was one move from the open column. [%cal Gh1d1] }) (14. Bxf6 { The bishop grabs a knight and trades off one of your own attackers. } 14... Qxf6 15. Rd1 { You keep the king attack rather than exchange your bishop for the rook on d7. } 15... Bd6 { Your rook reaches d1 anyway — at the cost of the bishop that had his knight pinned. [%cal Gd1d7] }) 1-0


[Event "Lasker plans: Use an advanced pawn as a hook"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqr1k1/pppnppbp/3p1np1/8/3P1B1P/3BPN2/PPPN1PP1/R2QK2R_w_KQ_-_1_2"]
