{ Now let us open lines against his king: a hook is an enemy pawn you attack to help prise a column open. Push to h5 and use g6 as your hook. [%cal Yh4h5,Gh5g6,Gh1h7] } 2. h5 { Your pawn attacks the hook on g6, and your rook on h1 is already standing behind it. [%cal Gh4h5,Gh1h7] } (2. c3 { A useful little pawn move. It does nothing about the pawn on g6, and that pawn is the hook your attack needs. } 2... h6 { A quieter game, and his king side is left in peace. [%cal Gh4h5] }) (2. O-O { Castling is safe — and it takes your rook off the h column, the one this plan is trying to open. } 2... e5 { His pawn takes the middle and the storm never starts. [%cal Gh4h5] }) (2. e4 { More space in the middle, fine in itself. It also hands his king side a free move to get ready. } 2... c5 { Solid, and no pressure at all where his king lives. The pawn to h5 hits the hook at once. [%cal Gh4h5] }) (2. Bg5 { The bishop comes out naturally, and the pawn storm waits a move — a move he is glad to use. } 2... c6 { Natural, and slow. Hitting g6 with the h pawn is what prises the column open. [%cal Gh4h5] }) 2... Nxh5 { His knight takes your pawn on h5 and plugs the column. The knight is the plug: take it with the rook. [%cal Yh1h5] } 3. Rxh5 { Your rook gives itself for the knight on h5, and the plug is gone. [%cal Gh1h5] } (3. Bg5 { You keep the rook, but his knight on h5 ate your pawn for free and still blocks the column. } 3... c5 4. c3 { You are a pawn down and the h column is shut. The hook plan needs the rook to take on h5. [%csl Rh5] }) (3. Bh2 { The bishop steps back and leaves his knight sitting on h5, a free pawn in his pocket. } 3... e5 { His pawn hits the centre, and your column is still blocked by the knight. [%csl Rh5] }) 3... gxh5 { His g pawn takes back. The hook has left g6, and the pawn on h7 has nothing guarding it but the king. Bishop to h7, check. [%cal Yd3h7] } 4. Bxh7+ { Check, and his king must run. Now watch. [%tail] [%cal Gd3h7] } (4. Ne4 { The knight comes first, and that gives his knight time to reach f8 and guard h7. [%cal Rd7f8,Rf8h7] } 4... Nf8 { His knight on f8 now guards h7. You gave the rook and got only a knight back. [%csl Rh7][%cal Rf8h7] }) 4... Kf8 5. Ne4 { His king has run to f8, the pawns on g6 and h7 are gone, and the h column is open. A rook for a knight and a pawn bought it. [%csl Gf8][%cal Gh7h1] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker plans: Pull the pawn off the rook route"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4rk1/3bqppp/2n5/p2pPp2/1ppP4/2P2NP1/PPBQ3P/4RRK1_w_-_-_0_18"]
[White "Student"]
