{ The f column is nearly open. Can you pull its last pawn away and make room for a rook? } 18. g4 { A second pawn lever pulls the f pawn off the rook route. [%cal Gg3g4] } (18. a3 { A quiet pawn move on the other wing. His pawn on f5 still blocks the column your rook wants. } 18... Ra6 { He lifts his rook and holds everything. The lever on g4 is what drags that f pawn out of the way. [%cal Gg3g4] }) (18. Ng5 { You clear f3 for the rook — but his pawn still sits on f5, so the rook has nothing to look at. } 18... h6 19. Nh3 { Your knight has to come back, and f5 still blocks the road. The lever removes the blocker instead. [%cal Gg3g4] }) (18. Nh4 { The knight heads for f5 itself. Just as good, maybe better — though taking that pawn with a piece leaves the column shut. } 18... a4 19. Bxf5 { Your bishop takes, aiming at h7 while the knight supports f5. Both captures are sound. [%cal Gg3g4] }) (18. h3 { A little air for your king. The pawn on f5 is still the thing standing in your rook's way. } 18... Rab8 { Quiet, and the column stays closed. The pawn lever on g4 is what opens it. [%cal Gg3g4] }) 18... fxg4 { He accepts the second sacrifice. } 19. Ng5 { Your knight clears f3, and the f column stands wide open behind it — right up to his position on f6. [%cal Gf3g5] } (19. Qf4 { The queen move leaves your knight on f3 under pawn attack. } 19... gxf3 20. Kh1 { You tuck the king away before collecting f3; a rook recapture is playable too. } 20... Rae8 21. Qxf3 { You recover a pawn for your knight. The queen recapture keeps your rooks together. [%cal Rf4f3] }) 1-0


