{ His pawn move left a weak square beside the king. Can you remove the pawn shield? } 4... Bxh3 { Your bishop sacrifice pulls the g pawn away from its king. [%cal Gc8h3] } (4... O-O { Castling is safe and slow. The hole beside his king is open right now, and holes like that get repaired. } 5. Nf3 { His knight joins the defense, and the moment beside his king has passed. [%cal Gc8h3] }) 5. gxh3 { He takes, and that is a mistake. Taking on f7 with check resists longer. } 5... Qg3+ { Your queen enters with check. The f2 pawn cannot take her: your bishop on a7 pins it to his king. [%cal Ga7g1][%csl Gf2] } (5... Qxe4 { That pawn was not free: his knight simply takes your queen. } 6. Nxe4 6... Nxe4 7. Bxf7+ 7... Kxf7 8. Qd5+ 8... Kf6 9. Qxe4 { Your queen is lost for too little. The checking entry on g3 keeps the attack alive. [%cal Rd5e4] }) 6. Kh1 { His king hides in the corner, and your queen can take the pawn on h3 with check. } 6... Qxh3+ { Your queen takes the h3 pawn with check. His king has no pawns left in front of it. } (6... Qh4 { Your queen steps back instead of taking. That lets his queen come to f3 and cover h3. [%cal Rd1f3,Rf3h3] } 7. Qf3 { His queen guards the king, and you are simply a bishop down. [%csl Rf3] }) 7. Kg1 { His king goes back to g1. Bring in one more piece. } 7... Ng4 { Your knight lands on g4 beside your queen on h3. His king has no pawn cover, and the bishop on a7 still pins f2. That is the price of the missing pawn guard. [%tail] [%cal Ga7g1][%csl Gg4,Gh3] } (7... Qg3+ { Checking again only repeats the position: his king steps back to h1 and nothing new has joined the attack. } 8. Kh1 Qh3+ 9. Kg1 { Round and round. Bring the knight in instead of checking. [%cal Gf6g4] }) 8. Nf3 8... O-O-O { Your king is safe on c8 and your rook comes to the middle. For the bishop you have two pawns and a queen and knight right next to his bare king. [%csl Gh3,Gg4] } 0-1


