{ His king fled to the queen side. Can you open lines toward its new shelter? } 11. a4 { Your pawn break follows the king to its new wing. [%cal Ga2a4] } (11. Rad1 { The rook takes the middle column. Useful — but his king has run to the other wing, and that is where lines have to open. } 11... c4 { A sound developing move. It is a pawn, not a piece, that opens a road to a king. [%cal Ga2a4] }) (11. a3 { The same idea one square shorter. It takes b4 away from him without ever opening anything. } 11... Rf7 { A slower version of the plan. The full push asks his b pawn the question straight away. [%cal Ga2a4] }) (11. b3 { This pawn holds c4 and opens nothing. His king is on the far side of it. } 11... Rf7 { Solid, and nothing has opened. The a pawn is the one that can reach him. [%cal Ga2a4] }) (11. b4 { Just as strong: the b pawn goes at his shelter too. This chapter uses the a pawn, behind which your rook already stands. } 11... c4 { A fine plan of its own. The a pawn opens the column your rook is already looking down. [%cal Ga2a4] }) (11. Bd1 { The bishop steps back to a quiet square while his king settles in on the other wing. } 11... c4 { The bishop has a square, and the wing where his king lives is untouched. [%cal Ga2a4] }) (11. Ne1 { The knight goes backwards to reorganize. There is a faster way: throw a pawn at his king. } 11... Bb7 { Reorganizing is fine, and it is a pawn that opens the road. [%cal Ga2a4] }) 11... Bb7 { He places the bishop on the long diagonal, aiming down at your king side. [%cal Rb7h1] } 12. axb5 { You open the a column — the road your rook on a1 is already standing on. [%cal Ga4b5] } (12. Ra2 { The rook doubles up behind the pawn. Patient — but the trade on b5 opens the column this very move. } 12... Nd6 { Also good. Taking now is the simplest way to open the road. [%cal Ga4b5] }) (12. b3 { A second pawn joins in. Useful, and the column stays closed while you build. } 12... Nd6 { Fine too. The capture on b5 opens it at once. [%cal Ga4b5] }) (12. b4 { The other pawn strikes. Strong as well, though it leaves the a column shut for now. } 12... Rc8 { A real alternative. Taking on b5 is the move that opens a road toward his king. [%cal Ga4b5] }) (12. Ne1 { The knight reroutes while the tension sits there. He gets a free move to settle his position. } 12... Nd6 { Not bad at all, though the open column is there for the taking right now. [%cal Ga4b5] }) (12. h4 { Space on the far wing, a long way from the king you are hunting. } 12... c4 { Playable, and the a column is still waiting for a capture. [%cal Ga4b5] }) 12... axb5 { His pawn recaptures. } 13. Rxa8+ { You exchange the rooks on the new open column. [%cal Ga1a8] } (13. Rac1 { The rook leaves the column it has just opened, and hands it straight back to him. } 13... c4 { You gave up the open column, and his rook is the one that can use it. [%cal Ga1a8] }) (13. b4 { The next lever, one move early. Trading the rooks first takes a defender off the board. } 13... Rxa1 14. Rxa1 14... cxb4 15. cxb4 { Playable, though trading first leaves him with one fewer piece to defend with. [%cal Ga1a8] }) (13. c4 { A pawn push while both rooks still face each other on the open column. } 13... Rxa1 { He trades rooks on the open a column before taking the c4 pawn. } 14. Rxa1 14... bxc4 { Reasonable. Trading rooks first is what clears the road for the pawn lever. [%cal Ga1a8] }) (13. h4 { A pawn on the far wing, while the real business is on the open column right here. } 13... Rxa1 14. Rxa1 { Nothing gained out there. Trade the rooks and he has one defender fewer. [%cal Ga1a8] }) 13... Bxa8 { His bishop recaptures. } 14. b4 { Your next pawn lever asks his c5 pawn a question it cannot dodge forever. [%tail] [%cal Gb2b4] } (14. b3 { The pawn shuffles one square. It holds c4 and never asks his pawn on c5 anything. } 14... Nd6 { Playable — and the lever on b4 is what forces him to make a decision. [%cal Gb2b4] }) (14. c4 { You strike with the other pawn. Also good, though it settles the pawns on his terms rather than yours. } 14... bxc4 { A fine alternative. The lever on b4 keeps the choice in your hands. [%cal Gb2b4] }) (14. Ra1 { The rook takes the open column again. There is nothing left on it now that the rooks are traded. } 14... Bb7 { Harmless. The pawn lever is what opens his position further. [%cal Gb2b4] }) (14. Rd1 { The rook switches to the middle. His weakness is on the queen side, where his king lives. } 14... c4 { Playable, and the lever is the move that makes the weakness. [%cal Gb2b4] }) (14. h4 { Another pawn move far away from his king. } 14... c4 { Nothing is happening out there. The b pawn asks the question that counts. [%cal Gb2b4] }) 14... c4 15. Qg1 { Your queen steps aside, and his b pawn stands with no pawn left that can ever defend it. [%cal Gf2g1] } 1-0
